# API Configuration — two backend services (see generated-docs/project.md
# §Data Source & Backend Integration for the full connectivity picture)
# Copy this file to .env.local and update with your values

# auth-api / BFF — session-cookie login, logout, userinfo, health
NEXT_PUBLIC_AUTH_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4424

# transactions-api — resource server (files, transactions, users, roles, file-sources)
NEXT_PUBLIC_TRANSACTIONS_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4423/transactions-api

# Auth: cookie-based session (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict "session" cookie
# minted by POST /v1/auth/login on the BFF). No credential env vars — the
# frontend never holds a token; the browser manages the cookie automatically.
# Both backend services trust the same session cookie.

# The browser never calls either service directly: the app forwards /v1/auth/* and
# /transactions-api/* from its own address to the addresses above, so no
# cross-origin call (and no CORS negotiation) is involved.

# Server-only overrides — PREFERRED over the NEXT_PUBLIC_* names above for every
# call the Next.js server makes, which is all of them (browser calls go to the
# app's own address and the server forwards them). Prefer these in a deployed
# environment: nothing inlines them, so the address a running server was started
# with is the one that takes effect and ONE build can be promoted dev → staging →
# production. A NEXT_PUBLIC_* value, by contrast, is compiled into the build
# output. When these are unset the NEXT_PUBLIC_* values above are used, which is
# convenient for local development. The automated end-to-end run sets
# AUTH_API_BASE_URL to point at its stubbed auth service.
# AUTH_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4424
# TRANSACTIONS_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4423/transactions-api

# Local development over plain HTTP only: opt out of the Secure attribute on the
# session cookie. Never set this in a deployed environment.
# BFF_INSECURE_COOKIE=1

# Session idle timeout — both values are in SECONDS, and both are optional: unset,
# empty or invalid values fall back to the working defaults shown below (30 minutes
# idle, with 60 seconds of warning). The app converts them to milliseconds in one
# place, web/src/lib/session/config.ts.
#
# How long the user may sit with no activity before the session ends.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1800
#
# How long BEFORE that end the "stay signed in" warning appears. It can never take up
# more than half the idle period, whatever it is set to.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SESSION_IDLE_WARNING_SECONDS=60
#
# MANUAL TESTING: to check the timeout behaviour without waiting 30 minutes, set both
# in web/.env.local before starting the dev server — e.g. 60 and 20 gives a warning
# after 40 seconds of no activity and a return to the sign-in screen 20 seconds later.
# Restart the dev server after changing them (they are baked into the browser bundle).
